Creating an Adobe InDesign .jsx script to transform a spreads document with multiple master pages into a single page layout while preserving margins, layout, positioning, and references to master pages can be quite challenging due to the complexities involved in page elements and master page dependencies. Below is a simple yet effective script that will help you get started on this task.

This script will:
1. Iterate through each spread, selecting all the items.
2. Duplicate these items to a new single-page document, attempting to preserve their layout and positioning.
3. Re-assign the master pages to each new page in as far as possible.

**Important Note:** This script assumes that you have a document open and that your master pages are consistently laid out (as a direct conversion might not always produce a 1:1 page layout, particularly if your spreads are complex).

You may need to adjust the script based on your specific requirements and the complexities of your InDesign file.

```javascript
#target indesign

var sourceDoc = app.activeDocument;

try {
    // Create a new document preserving the original document's settings
    var newDoc = app.documents.add();
    newDoc.documentPreferences.facingPages = false;

    // Loop through each spread in the original document
    for (var i = 0; i < sourceDoc.spreads.length; i++) {
        var spread = sourceDoc.spreads[i];

        // Collect all pages within the spread
        for (var j = 0; j < spread.pages.length; j++) {
            var page = spread.pages[j];

            // Determine the master page used by the current page
            var masterPage = page.appliedMaster;

            // Add a new page to the document for each page in the spread
            var newPage = newDoc.pages.add();

            // Apply the master page relevant to the current page of the spread
            if (masterPage !== null) {
                try {
                    newPage.appliedMaster = newDoc.masterSpreads.item(masterPage.name);
                } catch (e) {
                    alert("Error applying master page: " + e);
                }
            }

            // Duplicate items from the current page to the new page, attempting to maintain position
            duplicateItemsToNewPage(page, newPage);
        }
    }

    alert("Conversion complete.");

} catch (e) {
    alert("Error: " + e);
}

// Function to duplicate items from the original page to the new page
function duplicateItemsToNewPage(oldPage, newPage) {
    var items = oldPage.allPageItems;

    // Loop through all items in the old page
    for (var k = 0; k < items.length; k++) {
        var item = items[k];

        // Check if item should be duplicated (not on master page or locked)
        if (item.itemLayer.locked == false && !item.hasOwnProperty("getElements")) {
            var duplicate = item.duplicate(newPage);

            // Maintain the same position on the new page
            duplicate.move([newPage.bounds[1], newPage.bounds[0]]);
        }
    }
}
```

### Before Running the Script
1. **Backup**: Always make sure to back up your InDesign documents before running scripts that make significant changes.
2. **Custom Adjustments**: Depending on your specific document design and requirements (e.g., complex alignments, groupings, or items spanning multiple pages), more detailed scripting may be required. Monitor the results and adjust the script as needed.
3. **Functionality Limitations**: Due to differences in how spreads and pages manage their elements, some manual adjustments might still be needed after running the script.

This script should serve as a foundational tool, and further customizations might be necessary depending on the complexity of your specific InDesign projects.

